Draft v3 status. This version records your review of Draft v2. Decisions D1, D3, D5, D7, D8 and D9 are approved. D2, D4, D6 and D10 are approved in principle and revised here: evidence ceilings are now defaults with justified exceptions, the one-level limit on Conditional abilities is removed, Tank and Assassin suitability no longer depends on a formal role record, and review is simplified for an AI-assisted workflow with human approval. Naruto is the calibration choice for Module 2B and is not a confirmed launch roster. The original canonical work is the default primary medium.

1. Executive summary
   Module 2A defines how one canonical character becomes one Power rating (1 to 5) and seven Role Fit ratings (0 to 5), and how those ratings are earned, documented, reviewed, approved and revised. It does not decide rarity, matchups, ties, winners, schemas or code.
   Preserved without change [F]: Power 1–5, seven independent Fit ratings 0–5, integer ratings, the seven roles, the approved role weights, the deterministic formula, no distribution caps, one playable canonical version per character, prime and reasonably sustainable canonical state, hidden exact ratings, no guaranteed healer draws, no combat simulation or active abilities. I rechecked the formula by recomputing every Fit and Power combination for every role. The score ranges, distinct-score counts and tie rates in Section 2 all reproduce.
   What this draft changes or adds [R]:
   • Evidence quality replaces feat counts. Power and Fit are guided by evidence grades and default ceilings (A direct demonstration, B reliable inference or authoritative statement, C weak or speculative), not by a required number of wins against opponents of a given level. Ratings are outputs and are never used as evidence for other characters, which removes the circularity (Section 3.4, Section 7).
   • Healer self-regeneration is resolved. Self-only regeneration is Healer Fit 0. Trivial first aid or minor healing of others is Fit 1. Self-regeneration counts toward Power and personal durability (Section 5.6).
   • Scale is no longer a requirement. The top rubric levels describe quality under demanding conditions, not group size. A small-team strategist or captain can reach 5 (Section 5.2).
   • Role boundaries are re-drawn with one test per pair. This also fixes a real inconsistency in Draft v1: temporary protection was listed under Support while shielding was listed as a Tank signal (Section 4.2).
   • Rating order is clarified. Draft v1 said Power is rated first and Fit is rated without looking at Power, which cannot both hold for one rater. Fit is now rated first, with Power hidden, then Power is rated (Section 8).
   • A complete canonical version policy with Core, Conditional and Excluded classes, so Power and all seven Fit ratings describe one coherent version (Section 6).
   • A complete evidence standard covering sources, anime and manga differences, statements, inference, contradictions, outliers, sparse evidence, confidence and review (Section 7).
   • A batch workflow from universe setup to approval and revision, compatible with the immutable dataset version contract in Module 1 (Section 8).
   • Diagnostic thresholds are advisory. The 0.9 correlation and the average of 2 high-Fit roles are indicators to investigate, never targets and never reasons to alter canon-based ratings (Section 2.6).
   Corrections made directly from the formula: the tie-rate wording in Draft v1 mixed two different quantities (Section 2.5), and a claim that every role has a different best scorer was inconsistent with tied maximum scores in the same text (Section 2.7).
   Decision status: Section 9 records your review of Draft v2 and how each decision is treated in Draft v3. No incompatibility with Module 1 was found. Module 1 holds no ratings, stores an opaque dataset version, and requires that version to stay retrievable and unchanged for the life of a match.
