   Scores are bounded by role minimums. Power is never below 1, so no role score can be 0. The lowest possible score in each role is the Min score column above (for example 8 for Captain and 3 for Strategist, Support and Healer). A score of 0 is not attainable under the approved formula.
   Three behaviors follow:
   • Strategist, Support, Healer. One Fit level (17 points) exceeds the largest possible Power gap (4 levels × 3 = 12 points). A higher Fit therefore always beats a lower Fit, and Power only separates equal Fit. Example: Fit 1 with Power 1 scores 20 and beats Fit 0 with Power 5 at 15.
   • Captain and Assassin. Power can partly compensate for Fit. In Assassin, Fit 2 with Power 1 scores 33 and loses to Fit 0 with Power 5 at 35.
   • Attacker and Tank. One Fit level equals one Power level. Fit 3 with Power 2 and Fit 2 with Power 3 both score 50.
   2.2 Powerful but unsuitable characters
   The maximum for a Fit 0, Power 5 character is 40 as Captain, 35 as Assassin, 15 as Strategist, Support or Healer, and 50 as Attacker or Tank. No role gives an unsuitable powerhouse an excessive score. A Power 5 character with Attacker or Tank Fit 0 should be rare, because Power includes combat capability. If one appears, the rubric evidence should be checked, not the formula. No change.
   2.3 Specialists versus generalists
   Specialists gain the most in Strategist, Support and Healer, and gain modestly in Tank, matching intent. Example: Tank Fit 5 with Power 4 scores 90, against Fit 3 with Power 5 at 80. No change.
   2.4 Overlap between Power and the combat roles
   Attacker and Tank Fit are partly combat-strength measures, and Power is a combat-strength measure, so these roles may behave partly like Power contests. This is largely intended, since the design gives direct combat roles more Power weight. Draft v1 reported correlations (0.91 for Attacker, 0.19 for Strategist) from the missing sample [P]. These values are not used here.
   Recommended response [R]:
3. Keep the approved weights.
4. Reduce double-counting at the source: Fit is rated on role behavior only, with Power hidden, using the boundary tests in Section 4.3.
5. Treat correlation as a diagnostic (Section 2.6). A strong correlation in a real roster can simply mean strong fighters are good attackers.
   2.5 Identical scores
   If rating pairs were uniformly random (Fit 0–5, Power 1–5), the chance that two characters get an equal score in a role is 12.2% for Attacker and Tank, 5.1% for Captain, and 3.3% for Strategist, Assassin, Support and Healer. In the last group an equal score means an identical rating pair. In the first two, different rating pairs can produce the same score, because 10 distinct scores cover 30 combinations. Draft v1 described the 12.2% figure as identical rating pairs only, which was imprecise. Real ratings cluster, so real tie rates will be higher.
   Tie-breaking belongs to Module 3 [D]. Module 2 supplies Fit and Power separately so Module 3 can use either as a key. A 55/45 weight split for Attacker and Tank would remove all ties between different rating pairs, but it changes approved weights, and I do not recommend it now [D].
   2.6 Diagnostic indicators, not targets
   Draft v1 proposed revisiting weights if Power and Fit correlate above 0.9 after 50 characters, and reviewing rubrics if characters average more than 2 roles at Fit 4 or higher. Revised treatment [R]:
   • These are indicators that prompt a look, not triggers for change. They are reported with each dataset release.
   • A flag starts an audit of evidence quality: are ratings supported, and are the rubrics being applied consistently? It never starts a re-rating to reach a preferred distribution.
   • A high correlation or many versatile characters can be correct. Ratings change only on canonical grounds (Section 8.9).
   • Weights change only if the audit shows the formula, not the data, produces a gameplay problem, and then only through your approval.
   • Small samples give unstable correlations, so no diagnostic is read before a meaningful number of characters across several universes is approved.
   2.7 Unverified findings in Draft v1
   • The 14-character sample, correlations and per-character scores are [P] and are not reconstructed here.
   • Draft v1 said every role has a different best scorer, yet it also named three characters tied at 100 as Attacker. If three characters share the maximum, there is no single best scorer, so the two statements cannot both be true. The claim is withdrawn until the sample is rebuilt and checked in Module 2B.
   • The statement that no character dominates is also [P]. Role slots are used once, and the matchup structure is decided in Module 3, so versatility is not itself a flaw.
   2.8 Coarse integer scales and knowledge reward
   A 0–5 scale cannot separate two excellent characters, and decimals are not added [F]. Hidden Fit rewards anime knowledge most in Strategist, Support and Healer, where Fit decides outcomes. Whether visible rarity signals Power or Fit is not assumed here. Rarity is defined in Module 4.
6. Revised Power evaluation framework
   3.1 Definition
   Power is a character's overall canonical combat effectiveness relative to their own universe, for the single eligible canonical version defined in Section 6. It is judged by the threats the character can credibly defeat, neutralize or withstand, not by destructive scale alone, popularity or story importance. Power is the only rating where general strength counts. [F: the universe-relative standard is approved, Section 9 D5.]
   3.2 Power levels
   Level descriptions use capability, so a rater can compare against anchors without counting feats.
