   The level names are shorthand for capability bands, not rankings. Any number of characters may share a level [F].
   Power is not a win guarantee. Power is a broad estimate of combat effectiveness. A higher Power does not guarantee victory over a lower-rated opponent, and canonical outcomes need not form a perfectly transitive ranking. Counters, compatibility, circumstances and tactical advantages can produce legitimate exceptions, so a result that appears to contradict the ordering is not by itself evidence that a rating is wrong. It is recorded and weighed like other evidence [F].
   3.3 Decision rule
   The rater assigns the highest level whose description the character demonstrably meets in typical high-stakes performance within the eligible version, supported by evidence of sufficient quality (Section 3.5). The comparison is made against the universe's validated Power anchors (Section 8.3). Five evidence categories are checked for coverage and are not scored or added: offense, defence, mobility, utility and sustained performance.
   3.4 Ratings are outputs, never evidence
   The circularity concern is resolved by three rules [R]:
7. Judge the opponent's demonstrated capability, not the opponent's rating. If a character defeats a rival, the evidence is the canonical outcome and what the rival is shown to be able to do. The rival's Power rating is never cited.
8. Evaluate outward from anchors. Anchors are fixed first and validated against canonical outcomes (Section 8.3). Other characters are compared to anchors or to well-evidenced outcomes. If evidence depends on an unevaluated character, the rater forms a working judgment from that character's own canonical evidence, notes the dependency, and the dependency is checked at review.
9. Circular chains are cut at anchors. If A's level depends on B and B's depends on A, both are rated from their direct evidence against anchors, and the circular comparison is not used.
   3.5 Evidence quality standard for Power
   Evidence grades (Section 7.4) guide how high a Power rating can go. Evidence quality, rating and confidence are related but distinct: quality describes the sources, the rating describes the character, and confidence describes how sure the rater is. The ceilings below are defaults, not absolute limits [F in principle, wording R].
   Evidence base
   Default ceiling
   Justified exception
   Grade A: clear demonstration of reliable capability
   Up to 4
   Level 5 where corroborated by a second Grade A or an authoritative Grade B tied to a stated reason. A single overwhelming, defining performance with in-world acknowledgment can satisfy this
   Grade B: authoritative canonical statement, or reliable inference with a stated mechanism
   Up to 3
   A higher level where reliable canonical evidence clearly and specifically establishes it, for example an authoritative account of a specific capability that canon backs with a mechanism. A setting that states an ability instead of showing it does not by itself cap the character
   Grade C: reputation, vague claims, speculation
   Up to 2
   None. Weak or speculative evidence never justifies a high rating
   Justified exceptions. A rater may exceed a default ceiling when reliable canonical evidence clearly establishes the higher level. The record must cite the specific evidence, explain why it is reliable, say what would make it wrong, and carry at least Medium confidence. Exceptions are flagged for independent review (Section 7.10). If the evidence supports two adjacent levels equally, choose the lower, record the higher as the plausible alternative, and flag it. The lower level is never chosen below what Grade A evidence directly supports.
   3.6 Special cases
   • Unconventional abilities. Control, sealing, illusion or utility abilities count if they reliably neutralize opponents of the relevant level. Power stops at the level where the ability stops working against shown resistance.
   • Preparation and conditions. Power is judged in the ready state, meaning what the character does with one normal action of preparation. Longer preparation and conditional abilities are handled by the Conditional class in Section 6 and weighed on their actual canonical effectiveness.
   • Defence and sustained performance. A character who cannot be hurt or who outlasts opponents has Power evidence even without offense. Power is not the same as Attacker Fit.
   • Limited appearances. A character with few scenes is rated on what is clearly shown and reliably inferable. Fewer scenes lower confidence, not the rating (Section 7.8).
   • Inconsistent feats. Use typical high-stakes performance. A result that contradicts two or more independent demonstrations is an outlier: it is excluded from the rating and logged (Section 7.7).
