    The seven existing roles are retained without renaming [F].
    Suitability, not occupancy. Role Fit measures how suitable a character is for a role. It does not require that the character has already held that role. Canon that lacks an opportunity is not penalized, and reliable canonical evidence of the underlying capability can establish suitability. Raw Power alone is never Role Fit [F].
    4.2 Inconsistency found and fixed
    Draft v1 listed temporary protection under Support and shielding under Tank. It also excluded temporary shields from Healer. The same ability could therefore qualify as Support or Tank depending on wording. The rule is now the single question "who takes the threat?" (Section 4.3, Tank and Support).
    4.3 Boundary tests [R]
    Pair
    Test
    Resolution
    Captain / Strategist
    Does the evidence concern directing people in real time, or designing the plan and reading the situation?
    Captain: command, coordination, morale, decisions that move others. Strategist: planning and tactics, with or without authority. One event can support both, but each rating must cite which capacity it relies on. Rank and title are not evidence for either
    Attacker / Assassin
    Is the pressure open, sustained and aimed at whatever is in front of the character, or covert and aimed at a specific target?
    Attacker: initiative, sustained offense and finishing. Assassin: reaching and removing a chosen target. Speed, lethality and power alone do not establish Assassin suitability. Both can be high for the same character
    Tank / Support
    Who takes the threat?
    Tank: the character is the one hit, blocks, intercepts, redirects the threat onto themselves or holds an enemy in place. Support: the character gives allies a protective or enabling effect without being the target. A barrier thrown over allies while staying back is Support. Standing in front and holding the line is Tank
    Support / Healer
    Does the effect restore injury, illness or life, or does it improve capability and options?
    Healer: restoring bodily injury, illness, poison or life in others. Support: enhancement, sensing, communication, control, rescue, transport, and restoring depleted energy or resources. Restoring energy is not healing
    Power / Attacker Fit
    Does the evidence describe how well the character fights overall, or how well they create and sustain offense?
    Power covers all effectiveness including defence and mobility. Attacker Fit covers offensive behavior only. Raw strength, durability and speed do not raise Attacker Fit unless used offensively
    Power / Tank Fit
    Does the evidence describe the character's own combat survival, or their function as the one who takes threats?
    Tank Fit covers absorbing, intercepting and holding. Personal durability counts toward Tank Fit. Exceptional durability or defensive capability can support high levels when it would clearly let the character take or hold threats for teammates, even if canon never shows them protecting anyone
    4.4 Avoiding double-counting without extra complexity [R]
    • The same canonical event may support different ratings if it demonstrates distinct capacities. The rater states, for each rating, which capacity the event shows.
    • General strength counts only in Power. No Fit rating may use "this character is strong" as its reason.
    • Fit is rated with Power hidden (Section 8.6). No other mechanism is needed.
    • Overlap in Attacker and Tank is accepted as part of the approved design and is not corrected by shifting ratings.
11. Revised Role Fit rating rubrics
    5.1 Method
    The anchored evidence ladder is retained [R, favoured by your planning collaborator]. Raters match canonical evidence to written level descriptions, and evidence grades (Section 7.4) set how high a rating may go. It has no distribution caps: any number of characters may reach any level if their evidence meets the standard [F]. A checklist treats all ticks as equal, weighted sub-scores invent precision the source cannot support, and rank-ordering quietly creates caps, so none of these is used.
    5.2 Design principles: scale, opportunity, prominence and suitability
    Draft v1 mixed four different things. They are now separated [R].
    Concept
    Meaning
    Treatment
    Suitability
    How well the character would perform the role's function
    This is what Fit measures
    Scale
    How large the group or conflict was
    Evidence of difficulty only. Never a requirement for any level
    Opportunity
    Whether canon gave the character a chance to show the ability
    Absence of opportunity is not evidence of absence. Reliable canonical evidence of the underlying capability can establish suitability without a prior role record, within the default ceilings in Section 5.4
    Prominence
    How much page or screen time the character has
    Affects evidence confidence, never the rating itself
    Consequences:
    • Levels 4 and 5 describe quality under demanding conditions, such as strong opposition, severe constraints, high stakes or great difficulty, not the number of people involved. A solo or small-team success under extreme conditions counts equally with one in a large conflict.
    • No role has a minimum group size. Evidence of large-scale success is strong evidence, not a condition.
    • Fit 5 is a quality benchmark, not a rank. It means the character meets the highest standard of the role that the universe demonstrates, or an equivalent standard where the universe never shows one. Several characters may qualify [F].
