    5.6 Healer inconsistency resolved
    Draft v1 said self-regeneration does not count as healing others, yet allowed self-only recovery to receive Fit 1. These cannot both hold, and the role definition says Healer restores others. Resolution [R, matching your planning collaborator's recommendation]:
    • Self-only regeneration is Healer Fit 0.
    • Trivial first aid or minor healing of others is Fit 1, and larger genuine healing rises from there.
    • A character who has self-regeneration and the ability to treat others is rated on the treatment of others. The self-regeneration does not add to Healer Fit.
    • Self-regeneration is evidence for Power (defence and sustained performance) and for Tank durability.
    5.7 Role evidence rules and justified exceptions
    Self-only regeneration is Healer Fit 0 [F], and Power, speed or lethality alone are never Role Fit [F]. Draft v2's numeric limits for Tank (personal durability at most 3) and Assassin (speed or power alone at most 2) are replaced by the suitability evidence above, so a character is not penalized for lacking an opportunity to show a team role. Where canon lets a rater judge suitability from reliable capability evidence, higher ratings are allowed. Departures from the default ceilings follow the exception record in Section 3.5. These rules limit which kind of evidence counts, never how many characters may reach a level.
12. Canonical character version policy
    6.1 Principle
    Each character has exactly one Eligible Canonical Version (ECV), a coherent period or state of the character. Power and all seven Fit ratings describe that ECV and nothing else. Ratings never combine abilities from incompatible periods [F, with the classification below as [R]].
    6.2 What "prime" means
    Prime is the character's highest reasonably sustainable canonical state: the strongest condition the character can occupy for a meaningful stretch of story time, return to or maintain reliably, with ordinary preparation, without losing capability afterwards. It is not the strongest single moment, and it is not defined by age. A state counts as sustained if it spans multiple engagements or a defined period of the story, not one fight.
    6.3 Ability classes
    Every ability the rater considers is classified before it can support a rating.
    Class
    Definition
    Effect on ratings
    Core (C)
    Available on demand or routinely, repeatable, sustainable through a typical engagement, with no unusual cost, preparation or outside help
    Counts fully
    Conditional (K)
    The character's own ability, real and repeatable, but limited by time, cost, cooldown, terrain, preparation, a partner or risk
    Weighed on its actual canonical effectiveness, accessibility, sustainability, cost and reliability. It may raise a rating by whatever the evidence supports, including several levels for a regularly accessible transformation. There is no fixed numerical cap
    Excluded (X)
    One-time, unrepeatable, externally dependent, borrowed, non-prime or outside the version
    Does not count
    Normal operating requirements are not conditions. A healer who must touch the patient, or a sensor with limited range, is operating normally. A condition is something that makes the ability unavailable in ordinary use.
    How Conditional abilities are weighed. The rater asks five questions and records the answers: How effective is the ability when used? How easily can the character reach it? How long can it be sustained and what recovery follows? What does it cost in harm, resources or risk? How reliably does canon show it working? A form that is routinely available and used in important fights counts nearly as much as a Core ability. A rarely reachable, costly or unreliable form earns less. The rating reflects judgment of the evidence, and no formula converts conditions into levels.
    Inclusion test. An ability is included if the answer to all four is yes, or conditional if exactly one answer is limited:
13. Can the character use it again under similar circumstances?
14. Can they keep using it through a typical engagement, or for the duration the role needs?
15. Does it depend only on the character, and not on a specific other character or a one-off circumstance?
16. Does it belong to the chosen version?
    6.4 Policy by case
    Case
    Classification and rule
    Prime sustainable state
    The default ECV. Abilities in this state are classed as Core
    Regularly accessible transformation
    Part of the ECV, not a separate character. Core if accessible on demand and sustainable without harm that removes later capability. Conditional if time-limited, costly or risky, and then weighed on its actual effectiveness. It may legitimately raise ratings by several levels
    Temporary transformation
    Conditional. Weighed on how often it is reachable, how long it lasts, what it costs and how reliably canon shows it. Counts only for abilities it reliably gives
    Exceptional one-time power-up
    Excluded. One-off awakenings, desperate surges and fluke circumstances. If one later became repeatable, the repeatable form is classified under the rows above
    Repeatable partner-dependent ability
    The character's own ability, which needs a particular partner or participant but is an established, repeatable part of the character's method. Conditional. The rater records how the ability works if that partner is absent or replaced, because the draft cannot guarantee a partner. An ability that fails entirely without one specific partner counts for little. One where the partner's role is minor or replaceable counts for more
    External assistance
    Excluded. One-time or situational help from another character, supporting cast or circumstance, such as a rescue or an intervention. The test is whether the character can repeat it by their own method
    Borrowed powers
    Excluded while borrowed. Abilities belonging to another being, lent, temporarily possessed or granted by a third party. If permanently transferred and integrated into the character, they become part of the version from that point
    Summons and companions
    Included if the summon or companion is a tool of the character, available on demand and not capable of acting as a separate draftable character. Core if unrestricted, Conditional if costly or limited. If the companion could stand as a separate character, excluded
    Standard equipment
    Included if carried routinely as part of the character's identity and method. Equipment found, lent, one-time or needing scarce consumables is Conditional or Excluded accordingly
    Preparation-dependent abilities
    Ready state is what one normal action of preparation allows. Longer preparation is Conditional and weighed on actual effectiveness. For Strategist and Captain ratings, preparation is evidence of the planning or organizing skill itself
    Conditional abilities (terrain, time, ritual)
    Conditional. Weighed on how often the condition is met in canon and how effective the ability is when it is
    Self-sacrificial techniques
    Fatal or character-ending techniques are Excluded because they cannot be reused. Non-fatal but costly and repeatable techniques are Conditional
    Characters who permanently lose abilities
    The ECV is chosen from the pre-loss or post-loss period (Section 6.5). Abilities lost are excluded from the post-loss version, and abilities gained afterwards are excluded from the pre-loss version
    Substantially different abilities across arcs
    Treated as separate candidate versions. Choose one ECV (Section 6.5) and rate it alone
    To tell the three look-alikes apart, ask two questions. Whose ability is it? Another being's ability is borrowed or external. Can the character repeat it by their own method? If yes and it needs a partner, it is partner-dependent. If it happened once because someone helped, it is external assistance.
    6.5 Selecting the ECV
17. List candidate versions. Periods or states in which the character's abilities differ substantially, recorded in a short version register.
18. Check eligibility. A candidate must be sustained (6.2), canonical (Section 7), and have enough evidence to be rated at all. A brief or unclear state is not a candidate.
19. Choose the prime. Select the candidate with the highest reasonably sustainable overall capability. If several are comparable, prefer the one with the strongest evidence, then the one most representative of the character in canon, and record the choice.
20. Do not composite. Abilities from rejected versions are not carried into the ECV.
21. Record the reasoning. The register notes each candidate, why it was or was not chosen, and any disagreement.
    6.6 Evidence that may cross versions
    Character-level traits that persist across periods, such as intelligence, training, experience, personality and leadership habits, are admissible for Fit ratings as long as they do not depend on abilities the ECV lacks. A Strategist or Captain rating may use planning or leadership shown in another period if the skill plainly persists, with the source period noted. Abilities (powers, techniques, equipment) are admissible only from the ECV.
    6.7 Consistency check
    Every evidence item carries a version tag. The ECV check fails if any rating cites an ability outside the ECV, or if two ratings rely on mutually incompatible abilities (for example, an ability only available before a permanent loss alongside one only available after). Failed records return to the rater.
22. Canonical evidence standards
    7.1 Principles
    • Ratings rest on what canon shows or reliably implies, never on what a rater expects, prefers or has read in fan discussion.
    • No fabrication. A rater records only references they have personally located and checked. A reference the rater cannot find, or recalls only from memory, is marked unverified and cannot support an approved rating.
    • Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. It lowers confidence. It does not prove a lack of ability (see 7.8 and the two kinds of zero in Section 5.4).
    • Evidence is judged within the ECV (Section 6), apart from persisting traits (Section 6.6).
    7.2 Source hierarchy [R]
    Each universe's policy (Section 8.2) fixes which works fall into each tier. The tiers below set precedence, and the examples are generic.
    Tier
    Source type
    Use
    1
    Primary canon: the original work in its main published or released form
    Decisive. Direct demonstrations here are the strongest evidence
    2
    Official supplementary material approved by the creator, such as official guides, databooks and creator statements
    Supports and clarifies. May settle ambiguity in Tier 1 but cannot override a clear Tier 1 demonstration
    3
    Official adaptation content that follows the primary canon
    Usable to confirm Tier 1 content. Adaptation-only additions follow 7.3
    4
    Non-canon: spin-offs outside the universe's canon policy, games, filler declared non-canon, fan material, wikis
    Not evidence. Wikis and fan pages can help find a scene but are never cited as proof. The rater must verify against Tier 1–3
    Precedence. A direct on-page or on-screen demonstration outranks a statement. A higher tier outranks a lower tier on the same fact. A later official source supersedes an earlier one only if it explicitly changes the earlier fact. Otherwise the conflict is logged (7.7).
    7.3 Primary medium, adaptation differences and adaptation-only content [R]
    The universe policy names the primary medium (for example, the original publication). The default rules are:
    • When the adaptation matches the primary medium, either may be cited, and the primary is preferred.
    • When the adaptation changes an outcome or ability, the primary medium governs. The difference is logged.
    • When the adaptation adds material (extra scenes, extended fights, added abilities, new characters), it is adaptation-only content. Default: excluded from Power and Fit, unless the universe policy explicitly accepts it as canon. Where accepted, it is Tier 3, can supplement but not override Tier 1, and its use is flagged for review.
    • Presentation is not evidence. Animation spectacle, music, camera work and visual exaggeration do not raise a rating beyond what the events actually show.
    • Translation and edition differences. If a decisive line differs across translations or editions, record both and treat the fact as contested (7.7).
    7.4 Evidence grades [R]
    Grade
    Description
    Examples of the kind of evidence (generic)
    A: Direct demonstration
    The ability or outcome is shown in canon, the effect is clear, and a reviewer could verify it from the source
    A clearly shown engagement outcome, a shown technique and its effect, a shown act of leadership or planning with visible results
    B: Reliable inference or authoritative statement
    Not directly shown, but follows from Grade A facts through a stated mechanism, or is stated by an authoritative source with specific, credible grounds
    An in-world expert or official source describing a specific capacity with reasons, a Tier 2 source clarifying a Tier 1 fact, a capability that follows plainly from a shown ability
    C: Weak
    Vague reputation, boasts, rumor, ambiguous scenes, unexplained claims, or inference that stretches beyond the premises
    Villain bravado, rumor, off-hand praise without grounds, a single ambiguous scene
    Evidence quality, rating and confidence are related but distinct. The grade describes the source, the rating describes the character, and confidence describes how sure the rater is. Grade A, B and C have default ceilings, with justified exceptions, in Section 3.5 (Power) and Section 5.4 (Fit). An authoritative canonical statement is not automatically capped: where it clearly and specifically establishes an ability, it can support a higher rating with a recorded justification. Quality, not count, decides the grade. One clear, verifiable demonstration is Grade A, and five vague mentions are still Grade C.
    7.5 Demonstrated feats versus statements
    Evidence
    Treatment
    Demonstrated feat in canon
    Grade A if the result and conditions are clear
    Statement by the narrator or an official supplementary source
    Usually Grade B, and Grade A if it states a specific fact about an event
    Statement by a knowledgeable in-world character, with stated grounds
    Grade B
    Statement by an in-world character who is biased, deceived, boasting or lacks knowledge
    Grade C
    Statements of rank, title or reputation without detail
    Grade C, and used only as context
    Contradiction between a statement and a demonstration
    The demonstration wins unless the demonstration is itself ambiguous (7.7)
    7.6 Reliable inference versus unsupported speculation
    Reliable inference (Grade B) is allowed when all of the following are true:
23. The premises are canonical facts of Grade A or authoritative Grade B.
24. The mechanism is stated in one or two sentences. If it cannot be stated, it is speculation.
25. The inference extends no more than one step beyond the demonstrated facts.
26. No canonical evidence contradicts it.
27. A second reviewer, given the same premises, would reach the same conclusion.
    Examples of acceptable inference: a character who consistently defeats a type of opponent can handle that type of opponent in a new setting. An ability applied in a way its stated rules clearly allow. A rater should note that the inference carries Grade B and so is subject to the default ceiling for Grade B, with exceptions as in Section 3.5.
    Speculation (not evidence) includes hypothetical matchups, "what if" extrapolation, power-scaling chains across several comparisons, fan interpretation of unclear scenes, assuming growth the canon has not shown, and assuming any ability that "must" exist because the character is important. Speculation is not recorded as evidence and never justifies a high rating. It may be recorded in the notes as a reason a rating might be wrong.
    7.7 Contradictory evidence and outliers
    Resolution order for contradictions:
28. Check the tier, with higher tiers prevailing on the same fact.
29. Check directness, with demonstration over statement.
30. Check version, with evidence outside the ECV set aside (apart from persisting traits).
31. Check the weight of the pattern, with the consistent majority of independent demonstrations prevailing over a single exception.
32. If still unresolved, do not average. Record both facts, rate at the level that both readings support (the lower), record the higher as the plausible alternative, and set confidence to Low.
    Outliers. A result that contradicts two or more independent Grade A items is an outlier. It is excluded from the rating and logged with the reason, such as a handicap, exhaustion, ambush, deception or narrative convenience. The rater must justify why it is an outlier. Evidence is never excluded merely because it is inconvenient. If the supposed outlier cannot be explained, the contradiction is logged and the record is reviewed.
    7.8 Limited canonical appearances
    A character with little canon is rated like anyone else, on what is clearly shown and reliably inferable. Rules:
    • Limited screen time never caps a rating directly. Evidence quality does, through Section 3.5 and Section 5.4. One clear Grade A demonstration can support up to level 4.
    • A character with few demonstrations has lower confidence, not a lower rating.
    • For roles with no evidence, Fit is 0, recorded as an unevidenced zero unless canon contradicts the capability.
    • Where evidence supports two adjacent levels equally, take the lower and record the alternative. This rule is a tie-break for doubt, not a general discount.
    • If the whole record is too thin to rate responsibly, the character is held (Section 7.10) and not approved until more evidence is available. It is not rated by guesswork.
    7.9 Evidence confidence and uncertainty documentation
    Each rating (Power and each Fit) has a confidence level:
    Confidence
    Meaning
    High
    Supported by Grade A evidence, no unresolved conflicts, and the level is not sensitive to a reasonable alternative reading
    Medium
    Supported by a single Grade A item or Grade A plus Grade B, with minor ambiguity, or by consistent Grade B
    Low
    Grade B or C only, unresolved conflicts, sparse evidence, adaptation-only support, or a level sensitive to the reading
    Every rating record documents, at minimum:
    • The level, the ECV identifier, and the confidence.
    • The evidence items, each with source, locator (chapter, episode, volume or equivalent), tier, grade, and whether the reference was verified or unverified.
    • The ability classes used (Core, Conditional, Excluded) for any ability that affected the rating.
    • The plausible alternative level and what evidence would change the rating.
    • Any contradictions, outliers or inferences used.
    • Which unevaluated characters, if any, the evidence depends on (Section 3.4).
    The exact record format and field names belong to Module 2B and Module 4 [D].
    7.10 Review requirements and status of uncertain evaluations
    Status path: Draft → Evidence-complete → Reviewed → Approved. Only Approved records can enter a production dataset. A record that cannot reach Approved is Held.
    Situation
    Review
    Routine record: Grade A support, no unresolved conflicts, Medium or High confidence, no triggers below
    Automated validation, then human approval with the rest of the batch
    Power 5 or any Fit 5
    Independent AI review: a separate pass rates from the evidence log without seeing the first ratings, then differences are reconciled against the evidence
    Low confidence, an exception to a default ceiling, inference, contradiction, outlier, adaptation-only evidence, a disputed ECV, or dependence on unevaluated characters
    Independent AI review of the reasoning. The record is listed for human attention in the batch summary
    Unverified references
    Cannot be approved until verified
    Independent review differs by 2 or more levels, or the case is controversial
    Human decision, informed by a short disagreement note
    Independent review differs by 1 level
    Reconcile against the evidence and record the outcome. Escalate to a human only if unresolved
    Human approvers approve batches, decide important disagreements and rule on controversial cases. They are not required to re-rate each character, and no multiple-rater or formal adjudication procedure is required for every record. A random sample audit of routine records by an independent AI pass monitors consistency (Section 8.8). Uncertain ratings are not guessed into production: a Low-confidence rating either gains better evidence, is approved by a human with the uncertainty recorded, or is held. Test and sandbox datasets may include Draft records, but production datasets include Approved records only [R].
33. Character evaluation workflow
    8.1 Overview
    The workflow has two phases. Phase A is done once per universe and again only when its inputs change. Phase B is repeated for each batch of characters. Batches of roughly 10 to 25 characters from one universe keep review manageable and let consistency be checked while examples are fresh [R]. The exact batch size can be tuned.
    Who does what. Claude drafts the evidence logs and ratings. An independent AI pass reviews records where the triggers in Section 7.10 apply. Automated validation checks the data. A human approves batches, decides important disagreements and rules on controversial cases. Approved records are immutable and published only in new dataset versions [F in outline].
    Phase
    Step
    Output
    A
    8.2 Universe canonical policy
    Approved policy document
    A
    8.3 Power anchors
    Validated, versioned anchor set
    A
    8.4 Role Fit calibration examples
    Versioned calibration set
    B
    8.5 Establish ECV and gather evidence
    Version register and evidence log per character
    B
    8.6 Rate Fit, then Power
    Draft ratings with confidence
    B
    8.7 Calculate scores and run consistency checks
    Check report
    B
    8.8 Review and approve
    Approved or Held records
    B
    8.10 Assemble dataset version
    Immutable dataset version
    Ongoing
    8.9 Revision
    New record revisions and new dataset versions
    8.2 Establish the universe canonical policy (Phase A)
    For each universe, before any character is rated, write a short policy that fixes:
    • The primary medium and which works fall into each source tier (Section 7.2).
    • Whether adaptation-only content is accepted (default: not accepted).
    • Which supplementary materials count as Tier 2.
    • The universe's recurring supernatural or technical rules that affect how abilities are read, and the standard conditions for "ready state" (Section 6.3).
    • Default handling of story arcs and versions (how substantial change is recognized, Section 6.5).
    • Name, spelling and translation conventions for locating evidence.
    • The universe's role vocabulary mapped to the seven roles, where useful. For example, which in-universe jobs correspond to Support or Healer.
    The policy is reviewed and approved once, versioned, and referenced by every record rated under it.
    8.3 Select and validate Power anchors (Phase A)
34. Select candidates. For each Power level 1 to 5, pick one to three characters with abundant, clear, mostly Grade A evidence in a single stable version. Prefer characters whose evidence is easy to verify and whose level is unlikely to be disputed.
35. Describe, then place. Place each candidate by the level descriptions in Section 3.2 and the evidence standard in Section 3.5.
36. Validate for consistency. Check that the anchors' levels are broadly consistent with canonical outcomes between them. Counters, compatibility, circumstances and tactical advantages produce legitimate exceptions, so outcomes need not form a perfectly ordered hierarchy. Unexplained contradictions are reviewed. Anchor validation uses canonical outcomes and shown abilities, never previously assigned ratings (Section 3.4).
37. Resolve conflicts by returning to the evidence, never by choosing whichever level gives a preferred distribution.
38. Freeze and version. The approved set becomes Anchor Set vN for that universe. Characters in the anchor set go through the normal review in Phase B as well.
    Changing an anchor is a versioned change and triggers an impact review of characters rated against it (Section 8.9).
    8.4 Select useful Role Fit calibration examples (Phase A)
    Calibration examples teach raters what "dedicated member standard" and "benchmark" mean in a given universe [R].
    • For each role, choose one to three examples each for levels 3, 4 and 5 where evidence is clear and not contested, with brief written reasons. Where the universe has no example of a level, say so and use the rubric text.
    • Add boundary examples for the pairs in Section 4.3 (for instance, a character whose ability is clearly Support and not Healer).
    • Add a few low-level examples (0, 1, 2) for each role, especially unevidenced zeros.
    • Examples illustrate standards. They are not quotas, a ranking, or a template for distribution [F, no distribution caps].
    • A calibration example is rated through the normal process, and an independent AI-assisted review checks that it is rated the same way. Disagreement is resolved from the evidence and the rubric wording may be clarified.
    Calibration examples may be added as new characters are approved and are versioned like anchors.
    8.5 Establish the ECV and gather evidence (Phase B)
    For each character in the batch:
39. Open a version register and list candidate versions (Section 6.5). Select and record the ECV. If the choice is not clear, flag it for review before rating.
40. Gather evidence. Search the sources under the universe policy and record each item with source, locator, tier, grade, version tag and verified status (Section 7.9). Do not rely on memory or on Tier 4 material as proof.
41. Classify abilities as Core, Conditional or Excluded (Section 6.3 and Section 6.4).
42. Search for each role. For every one of the seven roles, perform and document a short targeted search. A role with no results becomes an unevidenced zero. This prevents roles being skipped silently.
43. Note dependencies on unevaluated characters and on adaptation-only content.
    The evidence log is neutral: it records what canon shows, not ratings.
    8.6 Assign Fit ratings, then Power (Phase B)
    Order and independence [R]. Draft v1 said Power was rated first and Fit rated without looking at Power, which cannot both hold for one rater. The recommended order is:
44. Fit first, with Power hidden. Rate the seven Fit ratings from the evidence log without seeing or assigning Power. This reduces double-counting through the Attacker and Tank overlap.
45. Rate each role independently, one at a time. For each role: state the core question, list the evidence that counts, apply the ceilings in Section 5.4, assign a level, record confidence and the plausible alternative. Do not let a high rating in one role raise another, and do not rate by "the character feels like an X".
46. Then rate Power against the anchor set using the decision rule in Section 3.3 and the evidence standard in Section 3.5.
47. Do not revise Fit after seeing Power except to correct a documented error. A revision must cite the evidence, not the Power value.
    For AI-assisted rating, Fit and Power are produced in separate passes, with the Fit pass done without Power visible. An independent review pass supplies the independence needed, with no second human rater required.
    8.7 Calculate role scores and check consistency (Phase B)
    Scores. Role Scores come from the approved deterministic formula only. Calculated scores are never edited.
    Automated and manual checks:
    Check
    Purpose
    Range and integer check: Power 1–5, Fit 0–5, all integers
    Data validity
    ECV check: every cited ability belongs to the ECV, with no incompatible pairs (Section 6.7)
    One coherent version
    Ceiling check: any rating above the default ceiling for its evidence grade has a recorded justified exception
    Evidence discipline
    Role-rule check: Healer above 0 needs the ability to treat others. Tank 4 or 5 and Assassin 3 or above need the suitability evidence in Section 5.5, and are flagged if they rest on Power, speed or lethality alone
    Role boundaries
    Double-counting check: no Fit relies on general strength as its reason
    Section 4.4
    Calibration comparison: ratings for similar evidence match the calibration examples
    Consistency across characters
    Anchor sanity: anchor ratings unchanged, and new ratings are broadly consistent with anchor comparisons, allowing for counters and matchup exceptions
    Stable scale
    Plausibility flags: for example, Tank 4 or 5 with Power 1, or Power 5 with Attacker 0
    Prompts a look at the evidence, never an automatic change
    Unverified and dependency flags
    Section 7.10
    Distribution diagnostics. The correlation between Power and Fit per role, and the number of roles per character at Fit 4 or higher, are reported with each release as advisory indicators (Section 2.6). They never change a rating by themselves. A rating changes only if a check finds that the evidence or the rubric was misapplied.
    8.8 Review uncertain cases and approve (Phase B)
48. Automated validation runs first (Section 8.7).
49. Triage. The record is classed as routine or flagged, using the triggers in Section 7.10.
50. Independent AI review of flagged records. A separate pass re-rates from the evidence log without seeing the first ratings for Power 5 and Fit 5, and reviews the reasoning for other flagged records. Differences are reconciled against the evidence. A rating changes only on cited evidence, never to fit a desired distribution.
51. Sample audit. A random sample of routine records is re-rated by an independent AI pass. The sample rate is a tunable setting. High disagreement widens the sample and prompts a look at the calibration examples.
52. Human approval of the batch. The approver sees a summary of flagged records, exceptions to default ceilings, disagreements and controversial cases, and approves or holds records. Humans are not required to re-rate characters.
53. Record the outcome, including dissent and accepted uncertainty. An Approved record is immutable (Section 8.10).
    8.9 Revise evaluations when new information appears
    Trigger
    Response
    New canonical material (new chapters, episodes, official supplements)
    Re-open the affected characters. Check whether the ECV changes, whether new evidence upgrades a rating, whether an unevidenced zero can now be evidenced, or whether the new material creates a new candidate version
    Error found (misread source, wrong version, unverified reference proved wrong)
    Correct with a new record revision and note the error
    New universe policy decision
    Review records affected by the policy, such as accepted adaptation content
    Anchor set or calibration change
    Impact review of all characters rated against the changed anchors. Re-rate only those whose evidence comparison changes
    Diagnostic indicators flagged
    Audit evidence quality only. No re-rating for distribution reasons
    Rubric clarification
    Check affected ratings. Apply only where the clarified rubric gives a different result from the same evidence
    Revision mechanics. An approved record is never edited. A revision is a new record that references the previous one, goes through the same review proportionate to the change, and takes effect only in a new dataset version (Section 8.10). Revisions are collected into periodic releases and not published one by one, which limits churn. Every revision records the reason, the evidence and the approver.
    8.10 Compatibility with the immutable dataset version contract
    Module 1 requires that the engine stores an opaque dataset version, never reads ratings, and that Match Resolution reads ratings from that same version. Module 4 keeps it retrievable and unchanged for active and past matches. The workflow supports this as follows [R]:
    • A dataset version is a frozen set of Approved records plus the scoring formula and weights version, the universe policy version, the anchor set version and the calibration set version in force. Approved records only.
    • Once published, a dataset version is never modified. Any change, including a correction, creates a new dataset version.
    • Matches that started on an older version continue to resolve using that version.
    • The version identifier is opaque to Module 1 and Module 3. The mapping from identifier to contents is Module 2B and Module 4 work [D].
    • Evaluation work in progress never modifies a published version. Drafts live outside it.
    • Characters used in a dataset version and the rarity assigned to them are separate concerns. Rarity tiers are not defined here [D].
    8.11 Keeping the process practical for hundreds of characters
    • Tiered effort [R]. Well-evidenced characters with unremarkable ratings need a light pass: evidence log, ratings, automated checks and sample audit. Only the triggers in Section 7.10 call for deeper review. Most of the effort goes to the highest ratings and the least certain evidence.
    • Reuse. Universe policy, anchors and calibration examples are done once and reused for every later batch.
    • Anchors first, then outward. Evaluate characters who appear in the same story arcs together, so evidence is gathered once.
    • Defer hard cases. Low-evidence characters are held instead of blocking a batch.
    • Reconcile in batches. Reviewers handle disagreements at the end of each batch, not continuously.
    • Track the work. A simple register lists each character's status, confidence flags and pending dependencies.
