2.  First universe: Naruto for the initial calibration sample. This is a calibration choice and does not confirm the launch roster.
3.  Primary medium: the original canonical work by default, with universe-specific exceptions in the universe policy.
4.  Handoff notes for Module 2B
    Module 2B must do, and has not been done here:
    • Use Naruto as the calibration universe (a calibration choice, not a confirmed launch roster). Write its universe policy and choose its anchors, then rebuild the 14-character calibration sample from sources, treating Draft v1's sample as unverified. All earlier correlations, scores and "best scorer" findings are [P].
    • Check the scoring model empirically on the verified sample, including ties, Fit and Power overlap and role differences.
    • Define evaluation data requirements, including the evidence record fields from Section 7.9.
    • Define the immutable dataset version contents and the mapping from the opaque identifier, including scoring, policy, anchor and calibration versions.
    • Define module interfaces (Module 3 reads Fit and Power separately, Module 4 stores dataset versions).
    • Define independent testing and acceptance criteria.
    • Complete the final decision register and the deferred considerations.
    Facts and constraints for Module 2B:
    • The formula, weights, rating ranges and preserved decisions in Section 1 are fixed unless a substantial problem is shown.
    • The score table in Section 2.1 has been verified. Equal-score probabilities under uniform random ratings are 12.2% (Attacker, Tank), 5.1% (Captain) and 3.3% (others).
    • Tie-breaking, rarity and matchup design remain out of scope until their modules.
    • The ECV policy, the evidence ceilings and the role boundary tests are the main items to validate with real characters. Watch in particular how Conditional abilities are weighed, justified exceptions to default ceilings, Tank and Assassin suitability judgments, partner-dependent abilities, and the Healer 0 and 1 boundary.
    • Do not adjust ratings to fix distributions. Record observed distributions as diagnostics only.
    • Every claim about a specific character in the calibration sample must carry verified references. Nothing should be written from memory.
